1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,927
Slide 3 illustrates GUEST VS.

2
00:00:02,927 --> 00:00:10,340
LANDLORD: The Desktop Schism. James, break down this landlord analogy for our students.

3
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:20,270
Think of Microsoft as the landlord who owns the apartment building: they control the foundation, the plumbing, the taskbar, and the Win32 kernel APIs on the left card!

4
00:00:20,670 --> 00:00:26,536
And for fifteen years, Google was just a happy tenant living inside the Chrome browser room!

5
00:00:26,536 --> 00:00:33,870
But then, Microsoft put Copilot directly onto the taskbar and added a physical Copilot key to new laptop keyboards!

6
00:00:34,270 --> 00:00:35,470
Exactly!

7
00:00:35,470 --> 00:00:38,556
Google looked at that and said: Wait!

8
00:00:38,556 --> 00:00:44,972
If users click Copilot on their taskbar, they will never open google.com again!

9
00:00:44,972 --> 00:00:46,940
Our ad revenue will evaporate!

10
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:55,740
So Google launched a counter-offensive on the right card: they built the Google App for Windows, intercepting `Alt + Space` right at the front door!

11
00:00:56,140 --> 00:01:01,371
But because Google is a tenant, they don't have native Win32 UI privileges.

12
00:01:01,371 --> 00:01:07,440
So they had to drag a massive 1.2GB WebView2 Chromium engine into that tiny search bar!

13
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:09,040
Haha!

14
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:14,870
It's like bringing a 2-ton diesel generator into your apartment just to power a single reading lamp!

15
00:01:15,270 --> 00:01:18,906
Platform friction forces architectural bloat.

16
00:01:18,906 --> 00:01:23,190
Let us examine the Trojan Horse mechanics on Slide 4.

